  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 22

Iteration 21 measured that `back-edge?` is not load-bearing on the source
suite and kept it, because that suite never compiled a call next to a
backward jump. This file is the corpus that measurement asked for. It is
not a new allocator, and it does not remove the predicate.

Do not overwrite 04-21.

## Why the source suite still does not span this

Kotoba `loop/recur` desugars to a synthesized recursive helper
(`frontend-destructuring-loop-test`), not a `:mir/jump` back-edge in the
same function. `string-contains?` / `string-replace-all` loops live in the
runtime helper; compiled `main` has no back-edge (iteration 21).
`let-composes-with-recursion` is a real call, not a TCO jump-back.

The corpus is therefore hand-written GMIR v3, compiled with
`machine-ir/compile-gmir` + `encode-mc-module`, executed with `run-code`
(same path as `dual-phi-program` in `isa_execution_test`).

## The function

`id` returns its argument. `count-loop` takes `n`, starts `acc` at 0, and
at the header: if `n` is zero, return `acc`; else call `id(1)`, add that
to `acc`, subtract 1 from `n`, jump back. `acc` and `n` are live across
both the call and the back-edge.

GMIR phi rules that bit: predecessors are explicit jumps (not
`branch-zero`); the instruction immediately before the join label is a
jump (no fallthrough); no critical edge.

## Two measurements

**It executes.** Judah, both ISAs, `n` in `{0, 1, 5, 50}` equals `n`. No
`KEXE_TRAP`. Tests:
`v3-call-plus-back-edge-is-routed-to-all-vregs` (kotoba-mir encodings plus
a back-edge scan of the allocated instructions) and
`a-call-and-a-back-edge-in-one-function-execute` (amu, real kexe).
kotoba-mir: 44 tests, 1001 assertions, 0 failures.
`isa-execution-test`: 18 tests, 671 assertions, 0 failures.

**Neutralising `back-edge?` does not change the artifact.** Production
policy is `[:all-vregs 8]` on both ISAs. With
`(with-redefs [mir/back-edge? (constantly false)])` the override ran
(21 instructions) and the policy stayed `[:all-vregs 8]`. The linear
scanner cannot complete this loop; spill-required fallback, not the
predicate, selects all-vreg.

The override was proved live before that second number was read
(iteration 21's check). Green-both-ways is not an unapplied redef.

## The recommendation is still a judgement

The predicate is **retained**. This corpus exists and executes, but it
does not license removal: neutralizing it does not change this program.
Removing it would still send every other call+loop through the scanner,
and `:cfg-liveness` is still `false`. The scanner has been wrong about
control flow once (iteration 18).

**Remove it against a call+loop the linear scanner can complete.** Not
against this one.

